<!-- =========================================
     SECTION 6: KEY INSIGHTS & RECOMMENDATIONS
     ========================================= -->
<div class="section" id="insights">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-label">Section 6</span>
    <h2>Key Insights &amp; Recommendations</h2>
    <p>Data-driven findings synthesized into actionable product and engagement strategies.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-grid">

    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon" style="background:#dbeafe;">&#128694;</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Step Count is the Master Engagement Metric</h4>
        <p>Steps and calories burned have a near-perfect correlation (r = 0.973). Step count explains far more variance in calorie burn than workout type or session duration. <strong>Product implication:</strong> double down on step-count nudges, streaks, and daily step goals as the primary engagement lever — they directly proxy calorie outcomes better than any workout segmentation.</p>
        <span class="tag" style="background:#dbeafe;color:#1d4ed8;">High Impact</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon" style="background:#dcfce7;">&#127947;</div>
      <div>
        <h4>83.1% Workout Rate — With Wide Seasonal Swing</h4>
        <p>Annual workout participation ranges from 77.5% (2018) to 86.6% (2019) — a 9.1 pp band. Monthly variation adds another 6.4 pp (Jan 81.2% vs Aug 86.5%). <strong>Recommendation:</strong> Time re-engagement campaigns to January and October lows; deploy seasonal challenge features in Q3 (Aug) to sustain momentum heading into historically weak Q4 start.</p>
        <span class="tag" style="background:#dcfce7;color:#16a34a;">Engagement Timing</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon" style="background:#fef3c7;">&#128161;</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Stress Rising — 2025 Hits 10-Year High of 5.64</h4>
        <p>Stress level has drifted upward from 5.33 (2015) to 5.64 (2025), a +6% increase over the decade. August shows a peak at 5.79. <strong>Recommendation:</strong> Launch or prominently surface stress-management features (guided breathing, mindfulness check-ins) in summer. Running users show the lowest stress (5.36) — a messaging opportunity to convert sedentary users toward aerobic activity.</p>
        <span class="tag" style="background:#fef3c7;color:#b45309;">Feature Opportunity</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon" style="background:#f5f3ff;">&#127774;</div>
      <div>
        <h4>December &amp; March Are Peak Activity Months</h4>
        <p>December (11,113 avg steps) and March (10,976) are the two highest step months. June (10,267) and January (10,176) are the lowest. <strong>Recommendation:</strong> Run New Year step challenges in January to capitalize on intent while lifting the lowest-performing month. Leverage December's naturally high activity with "year in review" social sharing to boost retention.</p>
        <span class="tag" style="background:#ede9fe;color:#7c3aed;">Seasonal Strategy</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon" style="background:#fee2e2;">&#128200;</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Workout Type Mix is Flat — No Dominant Preference</h4>
        <p>All 5 workout types are used within a 1.3 pp band (16.0%–17.2%). This shows neither consolidation around a preferred mode nor meaningful segmentation. <strong>Recommendation:</strong> Rather than over-investing in any one workout vertical, deploy a "variety score" feature rewarding cross-type participation — users who diversify may serve as the highest-value cohort for subscription upgrade campaigns.</p>
        <span class="tag" style="background:#fee2e2;color:#dc2626;">Product Design</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon" style="background:#dcfce7;">&#128164;</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Sleep Is Flat — But "Tired" Users Are More Active</h4>
        <p>Tired-mood users log the highest steps (10,749 avg) and participate in workouts at 82.6% — only 1.3 pp below Happy. Sleep difference between moods is negligible (Happy 7.02 h vs Tired 6.95 h). <strong>Recommendation:</strong> Avoid assuming "tired" users are disengaged; they may be high-effort users needing recovery-focused content (sleep tracking, rest day scheduling) rather than motivation nudges.</p>
        <span class="tag" style="background:#dcfce7;color:#16a34a;">Segmentation</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon" style="background:#dbeafe;">&#128101;</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Demographics Are Uniform — Minimal Targeting Levers</h4>
        <p>Age, gender, height, and weight show minimal differential in steps (max gap across age groups: 488 steps, &lt;5%) and workout participation (max gap: 1.0 pp). BMI is identical across genders at 24.6. <strong>Implication:</strong> Demographic segmentation is a weak targeting axis for this cohort. Behavioral dimensions (workout type, mood, stress level) offer stronger differentiation and should be prioritized in personalization models.</p>
        <span class="tag" style="background:#dbeafe;color:#1d4ed8;">Personalization</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="insight-icon" style="background:#fef3c7;">&#9989;</div>
      <div>
        <h4>Tuesday Engagement Peak — Target Mid-Week</h4>
        <p>Tuesday has the highest avg steps (10,799) while Thursday is the lowest (10,317), a 482-step difference. Weekends (Sat 10,537, Sun 10,445) underperform weekday averages. <strong>Recommendation:</strong> Schedule push notifications and challenge prompts for Tuesday to reinforce the natural peak; introduce "weekend warrior" challenges to lift the lower weekend baseline.</p>
        <span class="tag" style="background:#fef3c7;color:#b45309;">Notification Strategy</span>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>

